# An episode: the starting condition, what is in mind, and the judge, in one
# file. Nothing else is needed to run it.
#
# Two intakes are on the table and the judge wants e2. Only the numbers can
# get it there; no rule here or in the corpus changes.
#
# What used to decide it was the FOCUS pool: `<focus>` unattended everything
# the load had brushed in, then attended the EVENT intake(e2, mary) and the
# PARTICIPANT person(paul) at weight 9, so a corpus whose multiplier hung on
# the participant picked e1 and one whose multiplier hung on the event
# picked e2. Both halves of that are retired -- there is no pool, and no
# per-line scoring picking among a rule's matched applications.

fact +person(paul)
fact +person(mary)
fact +intake(e1, paul)
fact +intake(e2, mary)

rule <focus> = implies( { +intake(e2, mary), no focused }, { +focused } )
fact +lane(<focus>, setup)
fact +lane_order(setup, 0)

# The judge. Ordinary rules, in the same machine, subject to everything the
# loop does -- which is why a missing verdict is read as failure rather than
# as nothing having gone wrong.
rule <judge-ok> = implies( { +picked(e2) }, { +succeeded, +score(1) } )
